Adham drivers performance as kylo Ren is  easily one of the best parts of the Star  Wars sequels and ever since his debut in  the force awakens the character has  stood firm as one of the franchise's  most beloved villains but just how did  driver prepare himself for the part in  the wake of 9/11 a 17 year old Adam  driver postponed his acting dreams in  order to join the Marines prior to this  the actor had tried and failed to get  into the Juilliard performance school  during a TED talk he gave in 2015 driver  elaborated on his decision to abandon  the arts and join the Armed Forces I  joined the Marine Corps and I loved it I  loved bringing the Marines one of the  things I'm most proud of having done in  my life  driver's career in the Marines was cut  short when he was medically discharged  luckily however he reapplied to  Juilliard soon after and this time he  got in many years later driver used his  military experience to drive kylo ren's  physicality how he responds to things is  probably a bit physically quicker than  most kisses on you know it's on edge  there at war but that's not the only  useful experienced driver gained from  the Marines my experience from the  military was more in the relationships  actually that I formed then that kind of  camaraderie and like the work ethic and  working as a as a team the actor has  also explained how a military  environment can be similar to life on a  movie set there's somebody in charge who  knows what they're doing the director  and when they know what they're doing  what you're doing feels kind of exciting  and important and when they don't it  feels like a waste of time one thing  that many people assume about Adam  driver is that he can be a pretty  intense guy on set and while some of his  Star Wars co-stars are known for  enjoying a laugh or two on set driver  has garnered a reputation for being  almost as brooding as kylo Ren himself  in an interview with Radio 1 drivers  co-star John boyega explained I think  when he's on set as kylo Ren the dark  side of the force likes to capture him  sometimes speaking about their time  filming the last Jedi Star Wars veteran  Mark Hamill told Vanity Fair he's very  moody and intense I remember saying to  Adam I don't know how you work or your  technique all I'm suggesting is if you'd  like maybe we could go to lunch we could  get together and hang out  but driver insists this wasn't part of  his process he just likes to stay in the  zone between takes the actor told  vulture I like to stay focused on set  but it's not because I have a process  that I'm imposing on everybody else  sometimes you have to be more focused in  between scenes because it's pure comedy  in between takes one way driver did that  was by sometimes staying in character as  Ren after the camera stopped rolling  even though he's not usually a method  actor the fact is that kylo Ren is a  complex and serious character so for his  scenes jokes with drivers castmates had  to come second to his performance  drivers co-star domhnall gleeson later  explained there's enough going on  already without adding in knock-knock  jokes depending on the day Adam is  hilarious but he's always good starring  in a franchise as huge and beloved as  Star Wars is always going to come with  an overwhelming amount of pressure when  driver first began work on 2015's the  force awakens he found that dealing with  that pressure was a matter of simply not  thinking about it  well I think with the first one that was  more terror or denying that you know oh  there look there's a Millenium Falcon  you're like okay I put it out of your  mind and just focus on this story in a  different interview driver admitted that  it wasn't just the history of the  franchise that added pressure to his  performance but also the sheer scale of  the project he said that by the time he  had begun filming on the last Jedi the  level of Terror I think was different  the force awakens I kind of had no idea  what the end result is it was all new  and I'd never worked on anything that  size or scale before fortunately when it  came time to return to kylo Ren for the  last Jedi he felt comfortable enough  with the character and the world that he  was able to unwind a little driver  explained the second time I just knew  more so whenever you kind of know more  for me I relax a little bit let's be  honest kylo Ren has done some pretty  nasty things in his time there is  killing his dad for one of course but  his allegiance with the evil Snoke and  his desire for galactic domination  aren't exactly endearing either you  think there could be little doubt that  the guy is a real villain heck he's even  got the red lightsaber to prove it  but driver disagrees he said that while  preparing to play the role he looked for  kylos human side and tried to figure out  why he behaves the way he does  through JJ kind of hearing what he had  to say about him  tried to hopefully make him as human as  possible in an interview with Empire  magazine driver explained that part of  the reason for his character's  questionable morals is that he's a  fanatic he said when they think of their  actions as morally justified it makes  them dangerous and unpredictable I never  thought of the character as an evil  person driver also lays some of the  blame on kylos parents han Solo and Leia  Organa and their obsession with the  rebellions caused a cause Ben solo  didn't necessarily connect with driver  told Entertainment Weekly looking around  and not seeing yourself and not  identifying with what's around you  I think affects how we behave of course  it helps that driver considers himself  closer to the dark side than the light  as he once joked I live in the dark side  constantly reminding ourselves that  we're all going to die soon for an actor  as thoughtful and serious as Adam driver  playing a character as angry and  impulsive as kylo Ren meant tuning into  a certain mindset in the past driver has  said that he views kylo Ren as being  young impetuous and driven by  convictions that despite being strong  aren't entirely rational according to  driver that aspect of his character was  really brought to life for the last Jedi  something about the youthfulness of him  was not lost on in Ryan's writing when  first getting to grips with kylos  character driver realized the character  was at that point of early adulthood  where he had to decide who he wanted to  be and figure out his place in the world  so when playing him driver looked for  something beyond his own measured  demeanor and tried to draw out kylos  less mature and more impulsive attitude  and when it came to accessing the anger  he needed for kylos explosive bursts one  thing seems to have worked in particular  I just think about taxes and traffic and  I'm there even the best theater school  in the world can't prepare you for every  acting challenge and Driver struggled  with one major aspect of life in the  Star Wars universe the green screen the  actor told Seth Meyers that it can be  pretty difficult to get into character  as a fearsome villain when you're stuck  in front of a green screen on an empty  back lot but you're standing in front of  the screen and they're like trust us  your lightsaber is working yeah or  there's space behind you it looks really  cool one thing that helped driver better  connect to kylos world was actually  watching the final product driver has  said that normally he doesn't watch the  movies he's in as it makes him feel too  self-conscious and can interfere with  his ability to embody his character in  the moment however he made an exception  for Star Wars because he wanted to see  what kylo was experiencing while driver  was acting against those green screens  he told Esquire part of the impulse and  wanting to see it is because there's so  much visual going on that I didn't know  what was happening  driver added that seeing the force  awakens helped him understand the world  of Star Wars and reassured him that he  was on the right track so much so in  fact that he almost decided he didn't  need to watch the last Jedi the first  item on any Darksiders checklist is a  good old fashioned scary mask even if  it's meant more for intimidating people  than it is for say life-support and  while kylo ren's mask might help him tap  into his villainous side driver himself  found that the headgear was more of a  hindrance than a help when it came to  playing the role he even described the  costume as the hardest part of returning  to the character for the last Jedi  saying that working with the mask is  especially challenging because it badly  limits his vision is terrible who's in  the movie despite this driver also felt  that the mask helped him understand and  convey his characters internal conflict  for the force awakens the costume  department carefully decided which  characters wore masks because they  didn't want the looks to feel derivative  of the original movies driver said that  kylos reason for wearing one is that  he's trying to hide his insecurities  about living up to Darth Vader  he told GQ the person kylos pretending  to be on the outside is not who he is  he's a vulnerable kid who doesn't know  where to put his energy but when he puts  his mask on suddenly he's playing a role  in order to make kylo Ren work as a  character driver had to find a voice  that successfully conveyed his rage but  didn't come across as silly at the same  time so he worked with the sound team to  come up with the robotic voice that  would come from under the mask as sound  editor Matthew wood explains I actually  came up with a process to allow atom  driver to be very Method about it and  hear what his mask was sounding like  while he was performing we'd bring him  into the studio and he could get right  up on the microphone and get really  creepy about his process and hear it but  it wasn't just driver Inwood who had to  be happy with kylos voice would said JJ  Abrams really wanted people to feel it  he wanted that organic process and he  won in kylo ren's mask to portray that  sort of intimidation however once on set  Abrams apparently changed his mind about  the voice they'd worked on during one  interview drivers co-star Oscar Isaac  recalled that the first scene both  actors shot together was a torture scene  but things didn't go entirely to plan  you know we did the first take and Jade  came up and was like yeah maybe maybe  try something different like what  fortunately after that false start  driver was able to land on the right  voice and make high/low as menacing as  he needed to be as a six-foot-three  former Marine driver was no stranger to  the physicality required of a force  wielding warrior but he still had to hit  the gym to prepare for the role and he  made some friends doing so - Shane Finn  who owned a gym and Carrie Ireland  met the actor as he prepared for  shooting on the last Jedi Finn described  driver as one of the nicest guys who had  ever trained at the gym and said that  the actor made a particular effort to  thank the staff for their help during  his training drivers hard work paid off  to inspiring the last Jedi director Rian  Johnson to introduce the shirtless scene  that has Sims past into Star Wars legend  speaking with W Magazine jon said he  knew he looked good Adam looks so damn  good because he'd been training hardcore  for the past six months for those fight  scenes I'm like if he looks so good we  should put him up there  drivers shirtless scene lived on even  after the  we left cinemas thanks to a viral  sensation that came to be known as the  kylo Ren challenge fans internet lurkers  and even John Mayer posted photos of  themselves shirtless wearing the same  kind of eyebrow-raising high-waisted  pants that driver wore for the scene by  far the best part about being in Star  Wars is getting to play around with  lightsabers just ask you and McGregor to  begin with it was quite difficult not to  make the sound yeah because it makes  that nicer Shh  noise kylo Ren's own red lightsaber with  its unique plasma cross guard has  already become one of the sagas most  iconic weapons and drivers said that  this generation of Star Wars actors have  had the privilege of working with the  best lightsabers yet he told The  Telegraph this is the first time that  we're actually fighting with a whole  lightsaber - before it was just the hilt  with something like an antenna or a  green stick but this was  state-of-the-art  it actually sends off light drivers  commitment to lightsaber training earned  his co-stars respect - I was watching at  him and he gives us so for now every  single time but driver himself admits  that throwing himself into his fight  training was a no brainer  it's just rigorous in a great way and  these stunt guys are very disciplined so  you can't half-ass it lucky for the cast  mastering the moves for the force  awakens made preparing for the last Jedi  just a little easier speaking with  scream slam drivers said because we  started last Jedi almost a month after  we finished the force awakens that kind  of vocabulary was still in our bodies so  it made things easier a little bit  faster but the rigor of doing it was  maybe more intense for this one check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about your  favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

